Package: gcTMB
Title: Fitting Growth Curves Using the Template Model Builder
Version: 0.1.0.9001
Authors@R: 
    person("Emil", "Skindersoe", , "e.skindersoe@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0000-2172-1614"))
Description: Fits non-linear growth curves over time by maximum likelihood
    using the Template Model Builder ('TMB'), with covariates on the
    parameters and random effects, which are integrated out by the Laplace
    approximation. The growth models are the logistic (Richards), Gompertz,
    Hill, Weibull, normal and gamma curves, each with an asymmetry
    parameter, and a straight line for comparison. They are parameterised by
    the asymptote or the maximum growth rate, a time (of the maximum growth
    rate or of half the asymptote), a scale, slope or shape parameter, and
    the asymmetry, with identity, log, logit or inverse links, and can be
    truncated at a threshold time before which the response is zero. The
    response can follow a Gaussian, lognormal, Poisson, binomial, beta-
    binomial, beta or negative binomial distribution. Fixed effects are
    given by formulas for each parameter; random effects can be correlated
    across parameters, with several terms, nested or crossed grouping
    factors and random slopes; and the Gaussian and lognormal residuals can
    be autocorrelated within individuals, with first-order autoregressive
    (AR(1)) or continuous-time AR(1) structures as in 'nlme'. Starting
    values are found by a linearisation of the curves or a grid search.
    Inference includes Wald, profile likelihood and parametric bootstrap
    confidence intervals, small-sample t and F inference for Gaussian
    models, likelihood ratio tests with parametric bootstrap p-values, and
    pairwise comparisons of the curves with single-step multiplicity
    adjustment. The times at which the curves reach a level of their
    asymptote or a given response, and the maximum growth rates, their
    timing and the fraction of the asymptote reached then, are derived with
    standard errors by automatic differentiation and the delta method.
    Methods are available for predictions with confidence and prediction
    intervals, fitted values and residuals (Pearson, randomised quantile and
    normalized), validation and curve plots, simulation from the fitted
    model at the observed or new times and individuals, updating parameters
    for simulation studies, model comparison by information criteria, and
    the extractors fixef(), ranef() and VarCorr() compatible with 'nlme' and
    'lme4'. Four vignettes cover the growth models and their parameters,
    fixed and random effects, the timing and maximum growth rates, and
    autocorrelation and simulation, and an overview shows most of the
    features in one document.
